@h1 Emphasis h1@

Wit has exactly two inline marks, and only two: an underscore pair for _italic_
and an asterisk pair for *bold*. Two, on purpose. Restraint keeps prose readable,
and — just as important — it means a mark can never fire by accident in the
middle of your arithmetic, your filenames, or your apostrophes.

@@@pre
This word is *bold* and this one is _italic_.
pre@@@

renders as: this word is *bold* and this one is _italic_.

@h2 Why so few h2@

Because a mark must _wrap a token_, ordinary punctuation stays out of trouble.
You get italics and bold without escaping anything around them. A phrase can
span several words, so you are not limited to marking one word at a time:

@@@pre
The lens held _the last of the evening light_, and the flame *never wavered once*.
pre@@@

@h2 They wrap a token, not your punctuation h2@

A mark character with word characters pressed against it on the inside stays
literal. This is the whole reason emphasis is safe to leave on:

@@@pre
The product 3*4*5 and the file_name_here both stay plain text.
pre@@@

Neither of those emphasises anything — the asterisks and underscores are flanked
by digits and letters, so they are read as arithmetic and an identifier, exactly
as you meant them.

@h2 The apostrophe case h2@

A mark closes on its second mark character, and an apostrophe right after it is
just plain text:

@@@pre
the _keeper_'s lamp
pre@@@

closes the italic on the second underscore and keeps the `'s` outside it —
you get "the _keeper_'s lamp", not a broken run.

@h2 Marks at a paragraph edge h2@

A mark works at the very start or very end of a paragraph. What a mark _cannot_
do is span a blank line: emphasis lives inside a single paragraph, so a mark you
open must close before the paragraph ends.

@@@pre
_Alone_, she climbed the stair.

Morning came *at last*.
pre@@@

@h2 Bold and italic together — put bold on the outside h2@

You can nest the two, but the order matters, and this trips people up. Bold on
the _outside_ works; italic on the outside does not.

@@@pre
*_both bold and italic_*
pre@@@

gives you bold-and-italic text. The reverse order leaves the inner mark literal:

@@@pre
_*this stays broken*_
pre@@@

renders as _italic_ text with the asterisks left as plain characters inside it —
the bold never fires. So the reliable form for "bold and italic" is always
asterisks outside, underscores inside: @@@code *_…_* code@@@.

@aside(class note) 💡 Why the asymmetry? The parser resolves the outer mark first
and only then looks inside it. Bold-outside lets the inner italic still be seen;
italic-outside consumes its span before the inner bold is recognised. You do not
need to remember the mechanism — just remember *_outside is bold_*. aside@

@h2 When you need the node form instead h2@

The marks are shorthand for two nodes: @@@code @em … em@ code@@@ (italic) and
@@@code @strong … strong@ code@@@ (bold). Reach for the node form when the phrase
you want to emphasise itself contains the mark character — for example, to
italicise a @code snake_case code@ identifier, which the underscore mark will
refuse to touch by design:

@@@pre
@em snake_case em@ is emphasised on purpose here.
pre@@@

@h2 Common mistakes h2@

@ul
@li _Expecting underscores-outside to work._ Only @@@code *_x_* code@@@ gives
bold-and-italic; @@@code _*x*_ code@@@ leaves the inner asterisks literal. li@
@li _Trying to italicise an identifier or arithmetic with a mark._ It will not
fire, by design. Use @@@code @em … em@ code@@@ if you truly need it. li@
@li _Reaching for a backtick code span._ Wit has no Markdown backtick span;
inline code is @@@code @code … code@ code@@@, covered next. li@
@li _Expecting a doubled mark to emphasise nothing._ An empty pair (two
underscores or two asterisks with nothing between) stays literal — it renders as
the two characters, not as emphasis. li@
ul@

